## Three different things

Wicca uses three words that are easy to confuse. Getting them clear will help you know exactly what you are doing.

### Dedication

A **dedication** is a promise to study and practise Wicca seriously, usually for a set time. In many covens, a newcomer who has decided to stay is dedicated as a student, often called a **dedicant**, at the start of their formal training. It says: "I choose this path, and I will learn it." It does not make you an initiate, a priestess or priest of a lineage, or a member of the inner coven. In this coven's programs, dedication marks the end of the devotee path and raises you to the position of Dedicant (or your coven's name for it).

### Self-dedication

A **self-dedication** is a dedication made alone, to the Goddess and God, without a coven. It is the central rite of solitary, eclectic Wicca. Scott Cunningham's Wicca: A Guide for the Solitary Practitioner (1988) did much to make it widespread, offering a self-dedication rite and arguing that the gods, not a human initiator, are the true source of a Wiccan's standing. Earlier writers, including Doreen Valiente in Witchcraft for Tomorrow (1978) and Raymond Buckland's Seax-Wica, had also offered rites by which people could begin without a coven.

Self-dedication is a real and sincere commitment, and many lifelong Wiccans have never been initiated. It is not the same as initiation into a lineage, and honest solitary Wiccans don't claim that it is.

### Initiation

An **initiation**, in Wicca, is entry into the priesthood of a tradition and, in initiatory Wicca, into its lineage, by people who were initiated themselves. In British Traditional Wicca, only initiation makes someone a Gardnerian or an Alexandrian. Initiation is a later step, taken after a longer period of training, and the initiation module of this course prepares you for your coven's own rite.

## A year and a day

Many Wiccan covens and books speak of studying for **a year and a day** before initiation, or between dedication and initiation. The phrase has a folk ring: "a year and a day" was a common legal and storytelling term in medieval Britain, a full year with a day added to make sure nothing was cut short. In Wicca it means: long enough to see the whole Wheel turn once, keep all eight sabbats and thirteen full moons, and know whether this path is truly yours.

Your dedication may begin that year and a day.

## What you promise

A Wiccan dedication usually includes promises like these:

- to honour the Goddess and the God;
- to study and practise the Craft faithfully for a set time;
- to keep the sabbats and esbats, alone or with the coven;
- to live by Wicca's ethics, as you understand them;
- to treat your coven and its teachings with respect and confidentiality;
- to keep your own Book of Shadows.

## What you do not promise

A dedication should never include:

- a lifelong vow you cannot take back;
- obedience to a person in all things;
- money beyond reasonable shared costs (candles, food);
- sex or nudity;
- secrecy that would hide harm;
- cutting yourself off from family or friends.

If anyone asks these of you, stop and talk to someone you trust outside the coven.

## Can you leave afterwards?

Yes. A dedication is a sincere commitment, freely made, and you may later decide the path is not yours. Wiccans generally believe the gods understand this. If you leave, do so honestly: tell your mentor or High Priestess, return anything that belongs to the coven, and keep the confidences you were trusted with.

## A new name

Some Wiccans take a **Craft name** at dedication or initiation: a name used in the circle, or privately with the gods, that marks a new beginning. It might come from nature, from a god you honour, or from a quality you want to grow into. It is optional. Some covens give names; some let you choose; some do not use them at all. If you take one, choose with care, and avoid the names of gods themselves, or sacred names from living religions that are not yours.

## Are you ready?

You are probably ready to dedicate if:

- you have kept the daily devotion, more or less, for at least a couple of months;
- you have kept several esbats and at least one or two sabbats;
- you understand the difference between dedication and initiation;
- you know what your coven practises and are willing to learn it;
- you want this for yourself, not to please someone;
- the timing is right in your life.

Doubts are normal. Name them honestly; they are not a reason to wait forever, but they are worth talking about.

> **Your coven:** Ask your mentor what dedication means in your coven: what you will promise, for how long, whether you will take a name, and what will change afterwards.

> **Try this:** Write a page in your Book of Shadows: why you want to dedicate, what you hope for, and what you are unsure about. Keep it, and read it again a year from now.

## What's old and what's modern

- **Old:** the phrase "a year and a day"; dedicating oneself to a god, in many ancient religions.
- **Modern:** Wiccan dedication and self-dedication, from the 1970s and 1980s.
